  "question": "If $\\cos^{-1} x + \\cos^{-1} y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, find the value of $x^2 + y^2$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "Let $\\cos^{-1}x = \\theta$.<br /><br />Then $\\cos^{-1}y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta$.<br /><br />Thus $x = \\cos\\theta$ and $y = \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$.<br /><br />Therefore $x^2+y^2 = \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta$.<br /><br />Using the identity $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$.<br /><br />Hence $x^2 + y^2 = 1$.",
